  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "So a TLDR my suggestions are: \n\n•Make the dynamic bones limit disabled by default\n\nor\n\n•Increase limit\n\nor\n\n•Alert the player that this option even exists (Like a notification on startup that says \"Hey, dynamic bones dont work anymore! Wanna enable them?\")\n\n\nDetails:\n\nFar too many people have been talking about how their's and other's avatars have been broken.\n\n\"Dude, why is your hair sticking straight up?\" \n\nNo, its just the terrible option that disables dynamic bones. You gotta toggle that off dude.\n\nSeriously, this doesn't even improve the frame rate in game by any measurable amount. Why are our avatars (The one thing that actually makes this game successful) being targeted with updates like this? At the very least you could up the default limit. I totally understand if someone walking into a room with 400 dynamic bones, sure, block those. But don't screw up 99% of avatars.\n\nThe VAST majority of the game uses dynamic bones, and the majority of those players have higher than the default limit. \n\nNo-one even seems to even be aware that this option even exists. I keep having to tell people over and over to enable dynamic bones.\n\nI am all for optimization, but disabling features is certainly not the way to improve a game at all. The day we dont get proper avatars (Ex: we get little to no freedom on making avatars, and end up walking around as low poly shapes (exaggeration) ), is the day the game (Very likely) dies forever, and users will find another game of similar quality. \n\nIf someone wants that very slight, marginal performance increase, let them intentionally disable dynamic bones.\n\nI don't mean to be targeting the devs here, but it just doesn't seem sensible.\n\nI'm certain there are far better ways to be handling performance optimization. A thing to be striving for above all else is multi threaded processing, which no doubt would resolve the games performance issues out of existence.",
